WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.735
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Common.

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.070
Now, I want to point this out

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.738
'cause this is the first
opportunity, obviously.

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:07.006
And I know this is not why...

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:08.674
You don't do the work
for the awards.

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:10.843
But you do have an Emmy,

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:12.344
you've got a Grammy,
you've got an Oscar.

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:13.846
That's the EGO.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:15.848
-Yeah, I got a little --
a little EGO.

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.750
-You've got a little EGO.
But this is it, this is --

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.054
Now, you've thrown yourself --
your hat in the ring for a "T."

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:23.556
Would you be upset
if you had an EGOT?

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.225
[ Laughter ]

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.059
-I like the way you phrase that.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:29.262
I mean, listen,
I'm very grateful

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:31.664
for just being on Broadway, man,

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:34.900
and to be a part of the theater
community itself.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.303
That was -- that was a dream of
mine at some point,

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:40.105
and the fact that I'm actually
doing theater now

00:00:40.105 --> 00:00:41.274
is just like...

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:42.775
I'm just in the spirit of that

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.744
and I'm trying to give my best
every night.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:45.912
-So you really want it?

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:46.913
-Oh...

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.314
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:49.649
Nah, nah.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.317
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.452
But this is the thing.
This is the thing.

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:56.622
For me, also,
it's like, every...

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:58.958
You know, what you just named,
which I'm super grateful

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:00.793
to have those awards
you just mentioned.

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:03.128
But every -- whether
I was doing music

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:04.463
or whether I was acting
in a film

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:07.800
or creating a song for a film,
I just went in it

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.636
with my heart and soul, man,
and put my spirit into it

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.305
and gave my best.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.274
And whatever results happen,
that's icing on the cake.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:17.777
Once you're done, you know if
they say you're in the running,

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:20.146
of course you're like,
"Okay, well..."

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:23.983
And to be recognized
by the peers of like...

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:28.054
To be recognized by people in
theater would be amazing feat,

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.656
but I'm just happy right now
that I'm on that stage

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.992
with Stephen McKinley Henderson,
who's just incredible.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.827
-He's amazing.
He's been amazing forever.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:38.331
-And this incredible cast doing
Stephen Adly Guirgis' play.

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:42.802
It's just like, man, this is
stuff like I dreamed of,

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.470
but I didn't know
I would be able to do it

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.073
with this group of people
and at this level.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:49.241
So, just for people
to say, man --

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:51.077
my family to come see me on
Broadway.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.746
Look, some of my family
had never -- some of my people

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.916
who came from Chicago
had never seen a play,

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.418
and they came to see
"Between Riverside and Crazy."

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:02.088
So that, right there,
you know, is an award.

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.090
That's a victory for me, yeah.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.592
-You talk about your family.
I know you're close.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:09.261
Your mom was your first business
manager, is that correct?

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.097
-Yeah, she was.
-How'd she do?

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.566
-Well, she was great initially

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.602
because, you know,
my mother's from Chicago,

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:20.273
and she was -- she's a teacher,
but she also did corporate work.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:21.974
So she knew business
pretty well.

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.143
She got that Chicago hustle
in her, too.

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:27.647
So she was really good with
the business and made me --

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.683
and asked me to invest
in properties early.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.185
But eventually, it started
to become like,

00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:34.887
you know, your mother telling
you, "Aw, don't do that,

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:36.789
don't spend money
on this and that."

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:41.294
And the business manager went to
business damager after a minute.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.462
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.030
-How'd she take the news
when you --

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.865
When you moved on,
how 'd she take it?

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.300
-Aw, man, it was tough.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.802
But, you know, when a mother
loves her son,

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.771
she gonna eventually say,
"Okay, it is what it is."

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:56.075
But now, she sees the business
is flourishing even more,

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:58.811
and she's doing the work with
my Common Ground Foundation

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.847
and having great
pajama parties now

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.248
and having great
birthday parties,

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.684
so she don't care about
the business like that no more,

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:05.852
long as the money is flowing.
-That's good.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.520
That's good. The money's
flowing, she's happy.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.355
-Yeah.
-You mentioned --

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:12.591
You mentioned she was honest
when you were Tiny Tim

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:15.094
in the second grade,
in your performance,

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:16.595
and I'm assuming
she's honest today.

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.397
Has she seen you on Broadway?

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.532
And what has she told you
about your performance so far?

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:24.537
-Yes, my mother brought 198
people to see the show.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:25.537
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:26.572
That's a real number.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:27.906
-That's a lot --
That's a real number?!

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:29.408
-That's a real number.
-All in one night.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:30.542
-All in one night.
-I'll tell you,

00:03:30.542 --> 00:03:32.244
my parents have asked me
for tickets before,

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:33.746
but never that many.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:34.914
-198 people.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.749
-My parents don't know
198 people.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.417
-[ Laughs ] 198.

00:03:38.417 --> 00:03:40.252
Well, and she loved it.

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:42.255
She came to opening night also.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:46.826
And she's saying she may bring
back 150 for the last week.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.227
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.897
That being said, she told me
I did very good in the play.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.531
She said --

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:54.467
but she said -- but she said...

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.336
She also said, "You will be
great eventually,

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:59.005
but right now,
you're very good."

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.339
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:01.841
-That's a mom for you,
right there.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:03.342
-That's a mom, yeah.
-That's the right kind of mom.

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.011
Common, everybody!

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:07.880
"Between Riverside and Crazy"
is playing at the Hayes Theater.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.716 line:0%
we'll be right back
with Jonathan Lemire.

